  "result": "I found the bug and fixed it, and all 8 tests pass.\n\n**Root cause:** `save_order` in `shop/storage.py:17` loads the existing rows, drops any old copy of the order being saved, and writes the list back. The filter was inverted:\n\n```python\nrows = [r for r in self.load() if r[\"id\"] == order.id]   # before\nrows = [r for r in self.load() if r[\"id\"] != order.id]   # after\n```\n\nWith `==`, each save kept only the rows with the *same* id as the order being saved and threw away all the others. So every save wiped the file down to that one order, and `load()` only ever returned the last order saved. With `!=`, other orders are kept and re-saving an order still replaces its old row instead of adding a second one.\n\n**Tests:** I added `tests/test_storage.py`, since `Store` had no tests before. It checks two things:\n- Two different saved orders both come back from `load()` (this is the reported bug).\n- Saving the same order again after a state change leaves one row with the new state.\n\npytest isn't installed here, so I ran the suite with `python3 -m unittest`.",
